  <p>If you only want the arithmetic the calculator uses, skip the narrative guides and read the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>. If you want the limits on that arithmetic, read the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.</p>

  <h2>How Whistleblower Awards Work</h2>
  <h3><a href="/guides/how-whistleblower-awards-work/">The shared structure of the four programs</a></h3>
  <p>Start here if you do not yet know which statute fits. The guide explains the premise the programs share: an award is a percentage of money the government actually collects, not of the loss, and not of a judgment that is never paid. It then sets out the four eligibility ideas that show up, with variations, in each program. The submission has to be voluntary. The information has to be original. It has to contribute to a successful action. And, except under the False Claims Act, the collected amount has to clear a dollar threshold: more than $1 million in sanctions for the SEC and CFTC, more than $2 million in dispute for the IRS mandatory band.</p>
  <p>The second half is about what happens inside the band. For the SEC, the factors are codified at <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/240.21F-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">17 C.F.R. &sect;240.21F-6</a>. The IRS applies its own list and has historically paid toward the bottom of the range more often than the SEC. The guide also explains why the calendar is measured in years. SEC matters commonly run four to seven years from tip to award order, and IRS matters often run longer because the award waits on collected proceeds. The tax section covers why the award is ordinary income and what <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/62" target="_blank" rel="noopener">26 U.S.C. &sect;62(a)(21)</a> does with attorney fees. It does not compute anyone&rsquo;s tax.</p>

  <h2>The SEC Whistleblower Program</h2>
  <h3><a href="/guides/sec-whistleblower-program/">Form TCR, anonymity, and the award order</a></h3>
  <p>Read this when the conduct is a violation of the federal securities laws, including the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act provisions that apply to issuers. The guide walks through Form TCR, why the submission date matters when two people report the same scheme, and the rule that a preliminary tip can be supplemented without losing that date. Anonymous filing exists only if a lawyer submits the form. You still have to identify yourself to the SEC before it pays.</p>
  <p>It then covers what <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/240.21F-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rule 21F-6</a> treats as a strong tip: specific people and transactions, documents rather than a general allegation, information the staff did not already have, and a causal role in the case. The award process after collection runs from a Notice of Eligibility to a Preliminary Determination to a Final Order, which is public as to the amount and not as to the name. The anti-retaliation section explains <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/78u-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Exchange Act &sect;21F(h)</a>, including the limit from <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/16-1276" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Digital Realty Trust, Inc. v. Somers</em></a>: Dodd-Frank&rsquo;s employment remedy requires a report to the SEC. An internal-only report may still fall under <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1514A" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sarbanes-Oxley &sect;806</a>, which is a different statute with different remedies.</p>

  <h2>False Claims Act qui tam suits</h2>
  <h3><a href="/guides/false-claims-act-qui-tam/">A lawsuit, not a tip form</a></h3>
  <p>Read this when the fraud is a claim for federal payment. A qui tam case is a civil action the relator files in the name of the United States. It is not an online form. The complaint is filed under seal. The statute gives the government 60 days, and courts routinely extend that seal for a year or more while the <a href="https://www.justice.gov/civil/false-claims-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Justice</a> investigates. During the seal the defendant is not served, and talking about the case publicly can damage it.</p>
  <p>The intervention decision is the number the calculator is asking about. If the government takes over the case, the relator&rsquo;s share of the recovery is 15&ndash;25%. If the government declines and the relator litigates, the share is 25&ndash;30%, on a case the relator now has to fund. Both bands are in <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/31/3730" target="_blank" rel="noopener">31 U.S.C. &sect;3730(d)</a>. The guide also covers treble damages, the per-claim civil penalty, the kinds of schemes that produce most of the recoveries (healthcare kickbacks, defense overbilling, grant fraud), and the first-to-file bar in &sect;3730(b)(5), which blocks a later complaint about the same allegations. A lawyer is a practical requirement because the relator is a party in federal court.</p>

  <h2>Common misconceptions</h2>
  <h3><a href="/guides/whistleblower-misconceptions/">Five rules people invert</a></h3>
  <p>This one is for the reader who has already talked themselves out of reporting, or who is about to report in a way that weakens the claim. Each section names the myth and the provision that answers it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>You do not have to be an employee of the company involved. <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/240.21F-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">17 C.F.R. &sect;240.21F-2</a> defines an eligible SEC whistleblower as an individual who voluntarily provides original information. Outside accountants, contractors, investors, and former employees have qualified.</li>
    <li>Reporting internally does not, by itself, surrender an SEC award. If you reach the SEC within 120 days, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/240.21F-4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rule 21F-4(b)(7)</a> treats the internal report date as your submission date. Internal reporting can also be a positive factor under Rule 21F-6(a)(4). It does not, after <em>Digital Realty</em>, supply Dodd-Frank retaliation protection on its own.</li>
    <li>Anti-retaliation protection does not depend on winning an award. It does, under Dodd-Frank, depend on a report to the SEC.</li>
    <li>&ldquo;The agency probably already knows&rdquo; is not the originality test. Original information is information from your knowledge or analysis that the agency does not already have. A general newspaper story about an industry is not the same thing as the transactions and the documents.</li>
    <li>Waiting for a complete file can cost the submission date at the SEC and can cost the case entirely under the False Claims Act first-to-file bar. A preliminary TCR can be supplemented. A second qui tam complaint about the same allegations cannot.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What these guides will not do</h2>
  <p>They will not tell you that your facts qualify, and they will not estimate the probability that the government opens a case. The calculator&rsquo;s output is an illustration of the statutory band on an amount you chose, which the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> states in full. They also will not introduce you to a lawyer. The offices that take filings are listed on the homepage under <a href="/#where-to-get-help">Where to get help</a>.</p>

